mary", "oldValue": "Pilgrimage Church of Saint John of Nepomuk is a church building based in Czech Republic. A moderately significant entity in the historical record. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "The Pilgrimage Church of Saint John of Nepomuk, completed in 1736 and located in Zelená Hora, Czech Republic, serves as a testament to the Baroque architectural movement and the cult of Saint John of Nepomuk, a national saint of the Czech lands. Designed by architect Jan Santini-Aichl, this church exemplifies a unique blend of Gothic and Baroque styles, becoming a pilgrimage site due to its association with the saint known for his martyrdom and dedication to secrecy in confession. The church's octagonal design, symbolic of the heavenly Jerusalem, reflects the broader spiritual aspirations of the Early Modern period in Europe, making it a significant landmark in both religious and architectural history.

The church was commissioned by the Czech nobility in the 18th century and completed under the patronage of the Benedictine Order. It drew pilgrims from across Europe, particularly after the canonization of John of Nepomuk in 1729, who was revered for his piety and his tragic death, allegedly being thrown into the Vltava River by King Wenceslaus IV. The site became integral to the Counter-Reformation efforts, symbolizing Catholic resilience against Protestantism and serving as a focal point for the Catholic revival in Central Europe.

